In Diné (Navajo) healing ceremonies, a singer and helpers create a sand
painting on the floor of the hogan, working with colored sands, pollen, and
crushed stone. The figures are precise and traditional, often arranged with
radial or fourfold symmetry around a center.

## Not art to keep

The sand painting is not made to be admired or preserved. It is made to be
used. The person being healed sits on or within it, and the image is then
destroyed and the sand returned to the earth, usually before dawn.

## A doorway, not a picture

The painting functions as a doorway between worlds, a place where the ordered
powers it depicts can act. Its impermanence is essential to its purpose.

